export default {"G000224":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000224</code></p><h3>Big étale site of an algebraic stack</h3><p>Use the category fibred in groupoids over schemes defining the stack. A family covers if its underlying maps of schemes form an étale covering.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06NV\">Stacks 06NV · inherited topologies on fibred categories</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks/#tag-06NV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0788\">Stacks 0788 · comparison with the lisse-étale site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-0788\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000224\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000247":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Topological spaces · structure · <code>G000247</code></p><h3>Connected component</h3><p>For a topological space, a component is a subset maximal among its nonempty connected subsets under inclusion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004S\">Stacks 004S · connected components · Stacks 004S · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-004S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000247\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000226":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000226</code></p><h3>Étale topology on relative prisms</h3><p>On the relative prismatic category, use jointly covering families of completely étale maps of prism rings in the opposite direction. Over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(B,J)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, these come from <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-completely étale maps out of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B/J</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> whose reductions modulo <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> jointly cover the special fibre.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.08229#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Remark 4.5</a> · <a href=\"https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/scholze/prisms.pdf#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Construction 4.4</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000226\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000229":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000229</code></p><h3>Morphism of ringed topoi</h3><p>For ringed topoi <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℰ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathcal E,\\mathcal O)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℱ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathcal F,\\mathcal P)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, choose a geometric morphism <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℰ</mi><mo>→</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:\\mathcal E\\to\\mathcal F</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and a morphism of rings <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">♯</mi></msup><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒫</mi><mo>→</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^\\sharp:f^{-1}\\mathcal P\\to\\mathcal O</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. By adjunction the second datum can be specified as <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒫</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P\\to f_*\\mathcal O</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01D3\">Stacks 01D3 · morphism of ringed topoi · Stacks 01D3 · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites-modules/#tag-01D3\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000229\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000227":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000227</code></p><h3>Nisnevich topology on relative prisms</h3><p>On the relative prismatic category, use the completely étale maps of prismatic objects for which the corresponding family modulo <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p,J)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is Nisnevich. Each point of the reduced-modulo-<span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p,J)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> target lifts with unchanged residue field.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.08229#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Remark 4.5</a> · <a href=\"https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/scholze/prisms.pdf#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Construction 4.4</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000227\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000230":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000230</code></p><h3>Point of a topos</h3><p>A point of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is a geometric morphism <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Set</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo>→</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Set}\\to\\mathcal E</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Pullback along this morphism is the stalk functor at the point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Y4\">Stacks 00Y4 · point of a topos</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00Y4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000230\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000225":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000225</code></p><h3>Relative v-site of a diamond</h3><p>For a fixed diamond Y, take maps to Y from small v-sheaves. A family covers if it is jointly surjective as a family of v-sheaves. Apply the size conventions of Scholze §14.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07343\">Scholze, Étale cohomology of diamonds, Definition 14.1(iii)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000225\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000228":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000228</code></p><h3>Zariski topology on relative prisms</h3><p>On the relative prismatic category, use the completions of Zariski localizations of prism rings. A family covers when the corresponding opens cover the special fibre, detected on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B/(p,J)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> over a prism <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(B,J)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.08229#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Remark 4.5</a> · <a href=\"https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/scholze/prisms.pdf#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Construction 4.4</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000228\">Open shared record</a></p></article>"};
